syntax: bound how deeply the syntax tree may nest

The parser recursed once per nesting level with no limit, and lists such
as "a && b && ..." or "1+1+...", which it builds iteratively, still
produced a tree as deep as the list is long. Deeply nested input
therefore grew the parser's stack, and the stack of every recursive walk
over the tree afterwards (printing, Walk, any tool's own traversal),
without bound; the Go runtime aborts a goroutine whose stack exceeds
1 GB.

The parser now counts how deeply the node being parsed nests, across
statements, word parts, nested parameter expansions, arithmetic and test
expressions, the operands of "&&", "||" and "|" lists, elif branches and
ksh nested arrays, and fails with "syntax nests more than 1000 levels
deep" past maxNesting. Real scripts stay far below that depth.

Reproducer, before this change: a language server built on this parser
which walks the tree from several goroutines used more than 6 GB on the
300 KB script made of "echo ", 100000 "$(", "x" and 100000 ")".

